  "summary": "I built the click-to-plant garden in `src/main.ts`. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it or `vite build`, so I haven't seen it render or tested a click.\n\n- **Scene:** The ground is a large brown plane with a far edge, so there is sky behind it. It has a hemisphere light, a shadow-casting sun with soft (PCF) shadows, and a neutral environment light. The camera looks down at 35°, and the ground fills the lower part of the view.\n- **Picking:** Clicks are converted to a ray from the same camera numbers the scene uses and intersected with the y=0 ground plane. A click that misses the ground, or lands outside the ground rectangle, is ignored. This relies on the camera's `fov` being vertical, which I confirmed from the engine's camera code.\n- **Plants:** Each plant is a stem, four leaves and a six-petal flower, drawn as two instanced meshes (stems and spheres) so many plants cost only two draw calls. Growth takes 8 seconds from the planting time, so each plant grows independently and a later plant is smaller than an earlier one. A sprout is a short light-green stem with two small leaves. A mature plant is taller, darker green, with four leaves and an opened flower. Flower colour, flower size, height and leaf size are randomised per plant.\n- **Limit:** The scene holds up to 160 plants at once. Clicks and `__plantAt` calls beyond that are ignored.\n- **Overlay:** The top-left corner shows \"Plants: N\" and a \"Clear\" button that removes all plants.\n- **Globals:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a frozen object with `plantCount`, `plants` (`{x, z, growth}`, growth live from 0 to 1) and `ready`. `window.__plantAt(x, z)` plants exactly as a click would. `window.__EVAL_READY__` and `ready` are set once the second frame callback fires.\n- **`__plantAt` bounds:** It also rejects coordinates outside the ground rectangle (x from −30 to 30, z from −10 to 30). Only part of that rectangle is on screen, so a test planting far off-screen would still count.\n\nI avoided a top-level `await` so",
